<<~ ahu #/sigils>>

!! Sigils, each on its own line

<<~ stance "🏛️ 🗡️">>
<<~ mu Wand "ascii/* glyph/🜂 element/Fire">>
<<~ carry Detail "ascii/* ascii/!">>
<<~ kanawai "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119">>

An address in prose: lar://alias:grant@host/heading.angle.dynamic/path#/fragment and one
without an authority: lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/handoff#/shift-grammar-watch.

A SIGIL NAMES ITS OWN VERB. TiddlyWiki reads `<<~ set …>>` as a call on a variable spelled `~`, so
the verb binds as a positional argument and the base paints it like any unquoted value. The dialect
names it beside that reading rather than instead of it.

<<~ set hud="aim" mode="scan" stance="🏛️">>

<<~ oracle "a reading the house takes">>

A closer names the sigil it closes, which TiddlyWiki reads as part of the variable name. The form
repeats as held text, named by its own dialect's fence meta rather than opened a second time as a
live closer:

```memetic-wikitext
<<~/ahu>>
```

A BEARING ARROW STANDS BETWEEN TWO NAMED ENDS, and inside a sigil the base grammar's unquoted-value
rule would take its dash and leave the angle to the parameters region — two tokens, neither named.

<<~ lares aim from="lar://claude@lararium/the-verb.names.itself" -> to="?">>

<<~/ahu>>

